Product principleDefault output should be “What decision are we making?” + “Options” + “Recommended” + “Risks if wrong” + “What to ask next.”Keep explanations short, but make the consequences concrete (time, cost, reliability, security, compliance, quality).Treat the LLM as a navigator: it should not “do the work” by inventing requirements; it should force the user to choose intent and constraints first.A better default formatDecision: One sentence describing the choice the user is actually facing.Best option (why): One short paragraph + 3 bullet reasons.Alternatives: 2–3 options with “Use when…” and “Avoid when…”.Fast risks: 3 bullets (what goes wrong if they pick wrong).Next question: Exactly one question that unlocks the next decision.“LLM did it wrong” guardrailsAdd a mode like “Decision Mode” (or make it your default) that refuses to proceed without: goal, audience, time/budget, and “definition of done” (asked one at a time).Make Bobby label assumptions explicitly (“Assuming X… If not, choose Y”), so non-coders can correct the LLM instead of trusting it blindly.Add a “Red Flag” box whenever the user asks the LLM to decide something that requires org context (security policy, legal/compliance, real budget, user data handling).Where to implement this in your extensionYour architecture already supports multiple modes (fastscan, breakdown, comparison, followup) and format selection flowing from the webview into AnthropicClient.stream(...), so “Decision Mode” can be another mode or a new BreakdownFormat that changes the prompt template.You’re already passing domain, format, and devMode from the UI message into the streaming call, so you can gate certain outputs (e.g., non-coder defaults) purely by UI presets without rewriting the panel plumbing.